deepseek-coder:6.7b,sample_0108.c,CWE121,bad,01,True,True,16;22,18;22,CWE-120,False,True,True,True,False,18.6,"In the given code, the function `SNPRINTF` is used to copy a string into a buffer. The buffer size is hardcoded as 100, but the source string `source` is of size 100. This can lead to buffer overflow, which can be exploited to write beyond the bounds of the buffer, potentially overwriting adjacent memory, corrupting the heap, or even executing arbitrary code.","The fix would be to use the length of the source string to determine the size of the buffer in the `SNPRINTF` function. This can be done by adding a new variable to hold the length of the source string and using that in the `SNPRINTF` function. The corrected code would look like this:

```c
18  { 
20  char source[100];
21  memset(source, 'C', 100-1);
22  source[100-1] = '\0';
23  int source_length = strlen(source);
24  SNPRINTF(data, source_length+1, ""%s"", source);
25  printLine(data);
26 }
```"
